{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "fixed_environment",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "The target mapping is first-order RC discharge with electrothermal feedback, where resistor heating depends only on self-generated Joule power and cooling to a fixed ambient; thus dV_C_dt as a function of observed (V_C, T_R) is fully determined by the instantaneous resistor temperature and has no memory of capacitor temperature lag.",
      "child_relation": "Relax the assumption that the capacitor effective capacitance follows resistor temperature instantaneously and instead let the capacitor have its own thermal state that evolves with lag relative to the resistor. Then dV_C_dt depends on V_C, T_R, and the capacitor temperature state, creating history-dependent curvature in the discharge rate because the capacitance drift no longer tracks resistor heating algebraically.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "V_C",
        "T_R",
        "capacitance",
        "cap_temp_coeff"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "Across trajectories with the same instantaneous V_C and similar T_R reached at different times during discharge, the child permits different dV_C_dt values due to delayed capacitor warming/cooling, producing a measurable early/late-time curvature difference in voltage decay that the parent cannot represent."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "delta_TR",
        "expression": "T_R - T_ref",
        "role": "Resistor thermal excursion controlling present resistance nonlinearity in the parent"
      },
      {
        "name": "tau_elec_inst",
        "expression": "capacitance*(1 + cap_temp_coeff*(T_R - T_ref))*resistance*(1 + temp_coeff*(T_R - T_ref) + resistance_temp_quadratic_coeff*(T_R - T_ref)**2)",
        "role": "Parent instantaneous RC time constant implied by the algebraic temperature-following assumption"
      },
      {
        "name": "p_joule",
        "expression": "V_C**2/(resistance*(1 + temp_coeff*(T_R - T_ref) + resistance_temp_quadratic_coeff*(T_R - T_ref)**2))",
        "role": "Electrical heating rate that drives resistor temperature and indirectly reveals when thermal lag effects can accumulate"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when resistor self-heating is strong enough that T_R departs appreciably from T_ref during the discharge and the capacitor thermal equilibration is not effectively instantaneous compared with the electrical decay, so thermal lag can separate capacitor temperature from resistor temperature over part of the trajectory.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training, moderate self-heating already bends the decay through T_R; in holdout, longer or hotter trajectories amplify the mismatch between instantaneous T_R and the capacitance-setting temperature, extending the same lag mechanism rather than introducing a new process.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law in the limit of vanishing capacitor thermal lag, where the new capacitor thermal state rapidly equilibrates to T_R so capacitance again becomes an algebraic function of T_R alone.",
    "freedom_change": "add_state",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "Existing states do not encode any thermal memory for the capacitor. A separate capacitor-temperature state is needed because delayed capacitance response cannot be represented by refitting the current instantaneous function of V_C and T_R alone.",
    "gap_statement": "The model assumes the capacitor's temperature-dependent capacitance can be approximated using the resistor temperature as an instantaneous proxy, omitting capacitor thermal inertia and local temperature mismatch.",
    "baseline_assumption": "Temperature-dependent capacitance is taken as an instantaneous algebraic function of resistor temperature rather than the capacitor's own evolving temperature.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Finite thermal coupling and heat capacity of the capacitor create a delayed capacitor temperature, so the effective capacitance responds with thermal lag to resistor heating and ambient cooling.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "V_C",
      "T_R",
      "capacitance",
      "cap_temp_coeff"
    ],
    "rationale": "A natural next refinement is to separate the capacitor's thermal response from the resistor's. The parent already includes resistor self-heating and uses T_R as a lumped proxy for capacitance drift, which is reasonable only when capacitor temperature closely tracks resistor temperature. Here the varied condition is the strength and duration of self-heating along trajectories, while component reference parameters and initial V_C remain fixed under the experiment contract. Under appreciable heating, the parent predicts a unique target slope once V_C and T_R are known, because capacitance is instantaneously tied to T_R. The child predicts that two times with the same V_C and similar T_R can still have different dV_C_dt if the capacitor temperature lags: early in a discharge the resistor may heat quickly while the capacitor remains cooler, keeping capacitance nearer its reference value; later, the capacitor may stay warm even as resistor heating subsides, altering the decay slope. This is a genuine change to the scored target mapping, not only to an auxiliary trajectory, because dV_C_dt would depend on an additional evolving state. The effect is admissible when thermal coupling between resistor and capacitor is finite and resistor heating is non-negligible; if coupling is extremely fast or cap_temp_coeff is tiny, the refinement may be weak, and the parent is recovered."
  },
